  "outstanding_asks": [
    {
      "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
      "ask": "This makes the result depend on the state manager. A background task gets another state outside the lock, then writes it inside the block: ```python other = await self.get_state(OtherState) async with self: other.value += 1 ``` - Memory manager: the write succeeds. - Redis manager: `ImmutableStateError: OtherState is read-only outside of the event that holds its lock`. - Main: `get_state` outside the block raises on both managers. With redis, entering reloads the tree, and only `self` takes its place in the new tree. `other` still points to the old root, which the lock does not cover. So an app works in dev and fails in prod. Please make both managers behave the same. One option is to raise ",
      "addressed": false
    },
    {
      "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
      "ask": "This check runs on every tracked field write and every proxy mutation, so it is on the hottest path. Each call walks to the root, reads the ContextVar, and then `holds()` builds a generator for `any()` over `held.values()`. Please make the lock lookup O(1). For example, keep the held roots keyed by identity, so the check is one dict or set lookup.",
      "addressed": false
    },
    {
      "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
      "ask": "With redis or disk, this runs on every `async with self` enter and exit. Each time it walks the full MRO and rebuilds the slot names. `_stale_pickle_keys` already caches the slot names per class. Please use a cached per-class tuple here too.",
      "addressed": false
    },
    {
      "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
      "ask": "Active changes-requested review asks for consistent memory/Redis behavior when a state is obtained outside the lock, O(1) held-root lookup on the write hot path, and cached slot-name discovery.",
      "addressed": false
    }
  ],
